    "title": "Somalia's President Promises Democracy at UN, But Term Extension Dispute Lingers",
    "original_title": "Somalia president pledges democracy at UN amid term extension row",
    "slug": "somalia-s-president-promises-democracy-at-un-but-term-extension-dispute-lingers",
    "summary": "President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud told the UN General Assembly that Somalia is transitioning to multi-party democracy, while opposition leaders accuse him of ignoring a dispute over his term extension and constitutional changes.",
    "content": "Somali President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud addressed the United Nations General Assembly on Thursday, declaring that his administration is working to establish universal suffrage and move the country toward a multi-party democracy. He stated that Somalia is departing from the clan-based power-sharing system that has shaped its politics for decades, and that constitutional reform is underway to enable direct elections.\n\nThe announcement comes amid a continuing dispute over the extension of Mohamud's term and proposed amendments to the constitution. Opposition leaders in Somalia have accused the president of leaving these issues unresolved, raising questions about the legitimacy of the planned democratic transition. Mohamud's term was originally set to expire in 2022, but was extended by parliament, allowing him to remain in office.\n\nSomalia has been striving to rebuild its political institutions after decades of conflict and instability. The current system, based on a clan-based power-sharing formula, has been criticized for perpetuating corruption and inefficiency. Mohamud's pledge at the UN signals a potential shift, but the lack of consensus on term limits and constitutional changes has created political tension.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these developments are closely watched, as many have family ties and investments in the country. A stable and democratic Somalia could encourage greater diaspora engagement, including political participation and economic investment. However, the unresolved dispute over the president's term may deter some from fully committing until the political climate becomes clearer.\n\nThe international community has generally supported Somalia's efforts to hold direct elections, viewing it as a crucial step toward stability. The UN and other partners have provided technical and financial assistance for the process. Yet, without broad political agreement, the transition could face obstacles.\n\nAs Somalia navigates this critical period, the diaspora remains hopeful but cautious. The outcome of the term extension row and constitutional reforms will significantly impact the country's democratic trajectory and its relations with the global Somali community.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-president-democracy-un-term-extension-row/",
    "source": "somaliguardian.com",
