    "id": "news-1790345922931-2",
    "title": "Ethiopia, Djibouti, and Dangote to Build $660 Million Fuel Pipeline",
    "original_title": "Itoobiya, Jabuuti iyo Dangote oo dhisaya dhuun shidaal oo $660 milyan ah",
    "slug": "ethiopia-djibouti-and-dangote-to-build-660-million-fuel-pipeline",
    "summary": "Ethiopia, Djibouti, and Nigeria's Dangote Group plan to construct a $660 million fuel pipeline to enhance supply and reduce transport costs.",
    "content": "Ethiopia, Djibouti, and the Dangote Group, owned by Nigerian businessman Aliko Dangote, have announced plans to jointly build a fuel pipeline valued at approximately $660 million. The project aims to connect the two countries and improve fuel supply while reducing costs and delays associated with trucking fuel along the trade corridor between Ethiopia and Djibouti.\n\nThe pipeline is expected to span nearly 120 kilometers and will transport refined fuel. This initiative comes amid longstanding challenges in fuel logistics between landlocked Ethiopia and its port neighbor Djibouti, which handles the majority of Ethiopia's imports. The involvement of Dangote Group marks a significant private sector investment in regional infrastructure.\n\nAliko Dangote, Africa's richest man, has been expanding his conglomerate's footprint across the continent, with interests in cement, sugar, and petroleum refining. The Dangote Group's participation could bring both capital and expertise to the project, which is still in the planning stages. Neither the timeline nor the financing structure has been fully disclosed.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is noteworthy due to the broader economic ties between the Horn of Africa and global markets. Many Somalis rely on trade routes that pass through Djibouti and Ethiopia, and improved fuel infrastructure could lower transportation costs for goods, potentially benefiting businesses and consumers in Somalia and among diaspora communities engaged in cross-border trade.\n\nAdditionally, the project underscores growing investment in regional connectivity, which may create new opportunities for Somali entrepreneurs and investors. As the plan progresses, stakeholders will be watching for details on procurement, employment, and potential spillover effects on neighboring economies.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/25/itoobiya-jabuuti-iyo-dangote-oo-dhisaya-dhuun-shidaal-oo-660-milyan-ah/",
